  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nOur approach leverages evolutionary computation with adaptive dimensionality to overcome the limitations of gradient-based optimization and local search. The Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem is inherently high-dimensional and non-convex, making it challenging for traditional optimization algorithms. We combine: 1) Representing h as a binary vector for feasibility (0/1 values) and adaptive resolution (using parent transfer), 2) Population-based evolution with local search, 3) Kernel-based crossover and mutation, and 4) Multi-resolution trial. \n\nThe key innovation is dynamically switching between parent-based repair and self-contained initialization, combined with exponential time allocation to gradually find a C \u2264 0.3808. We focus on binary representations because the problem's constraints (0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 and sum(h) = n/2) lead to better geometric resolution in combinatorial space.\n\nMathematical specification\nVariables:\n- h: Binary vector of length n_points (initially 310)\n- Integral constraint: sum(h) = n_points/2 (each element is 0 or 1)\n- Objective: C = dx * max_lag(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full'))\n  with dx=2.0/n_points\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Repair function (Section 2.2) ensures constraint: \n   - First clip/clamp to [0,1] (naturally binary)\n   - Adjust sum by swapping positions while maintaining binary form\n2. Objective function (Section 2.3) computes described integral\n3. Initialization (Section 2.4) combines parent transfer and combinatorial structured patterns\n4. Mutation/Recombination (Section 2.5):\n   - Mutation uses exponential perturbation of least-squares-descent directions\n   - Recombination uses binary crossover with tournament selection\n5. Validation pipeline (Section 2.6) ensures 0\u2264h[i]\u22641, sum(h)\u2248n/2, and finite objective\n\nTime budget\n- 10s: Initial repair and validation\n- 100s: Pattern-based initialization at current resolution\n- 790s: Evolutionary optimization with adaptive resolution toggling\n- 100s: Finale optimization with parent reduction\n\nValidation and return contract\n- Check h[i] \u2208 [0,1] and |sum(h) - n_points/2| < 3\n- Ensure np.max(correlation) is finite\n- Return candidate from final SLSQP polish (fallback is repair(subset(initial,)) if constraints fail)\n\nresolution: 310 -> 620 -> 1240 dimensional jumps implemented as resampling with parental guidance (Section 3.3)",
